    "---\n",
    "title: \"The relationship between canopy height, topography, and residual spatial structure in LiDAR-derived canopy surface complexity in eastern Mount Rainier National Park\"\n",
    "author: \"Lavran Pagano\"\n",
    "abstract: |\n",
    "  The relationship between canopy surface complexity, canopy height, elevation, and topographic heat load was analyzed across a 7.4 × 16.7 km LiDAR footprint located on the eastern edge of Mount Rainier National Park. Airborne laser scanning (ALS) point clouds were used to derive canopy and topographic attributes across the footprint. Bayesian regression models were then fit to evaluate marginal and conditional relationships between rumple and the measured covariates. Residual spatial dependence was assessed using Moran’s I and empirical semivariograms, and an intrinsic conditional autoregressive (ICAR) spatial random effect was subsequently used to model spatial structure explicitly. Across both the nonspatial and spatial models, canopy height was the dominant measured correlate of canopy surface complexity. Although rumple showed a strong negative marginal relationship with elevation, this relationship substantially weakened once canopy height was included, and its credible interval spanned zero once spatial structure was modeled. Topographic heat load showed only a weak conditional association. These findings suggest that the apparent elevational gradient in canopy surface complexity is explained by the concurrent decline in canopy stature, consistent with previous studies linking canopy height to structural complexity. They also illustrate that the elevation coefficient, unlike the canopy-height coefficient, depends materially on how residual spatial structure is modeled.\n",
    "\n",
