    "# Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "In U.S. Pacific Northwest conifer forests, LiDAR-derived measures of canopy complexity have been shown to correspond closely with field-based assessments of stand structure (Kane et al. 2010a). A common measure of canopy-surface complexity is the rumple index. The rumple index describes the unevenness of the upper canopy surface as the ratio of canopy surface area to projected ground area, with higher values indicating a rougher canopy (Kane et al. 2010a).\n",
    "\n",
    "Previous research on canopy complexity has documented negative relationships between elevation and canopy complexity in both temperate and tropical forests. Across an elevational gradient in Great Smoky Mountains National Park, Walter et al. (2021) found that canopy height and internal canopy structural complexity declined with elevation. Moreover, in Puerto Rico, Ankori-Karlinsky et al. (2024) likewise found that canopy surface rugosity decreased with elevation in a model that included canopy height; their model predicted approximately 15% lower rugosity in forests above 1,000 meters than in forests below that elevation. Although these studies measured different dimensions of canopy complexity, both report a decline in complexity with increasing elevation. This pattern is not universal, however. In western Washington conifer forests, Kane et al. (2010b) found no consistent relationship between elevation and LiDAR-derived structural classes; the one exception was that primary mountain hemlock plots, in their highest-elevation zone, were structurally complex. Those classes, however, were constructed from rumple, canopy height, and canopy density to characterize stand development; the study did not test whether rumple itself varied with elevation independently of canopy height. Together, these studies measured different dimensions of canopy complexity and found different elevational patterns, motivating examination of whether the elevation–rumple relationship persists after accounting for canopy height and topographic exposure in eastern Mount Rainier.\n",
    "\n",
    "Decreasing canopy height may partly explain the decline in complexity with increasing elevation, as Atkins et al. (2022) found positive relationships between canopy height and structural complexity, with the form of these relationships differing among forest types and complexity metrics; in evergreen needleleaf forests, complexity increased less steeply with height than in other forest types. Taller canopies provide more vertical space for structural variation to develop. Consequently, where canopy height decreases with elevation, an accompanying decline in complexity may partly reflect changes in canopy stature. Accounting for canopy height therefore lets us evaluate the elevation–complexity relationship among canopies of comparable height.\n",
    "\n",
    "Another potential environmental predictor is topographic heat load. Calculated from slope, aspect, and latitude, the index represents potential heating associated with terrain exposure, from the coolest northeast-facing to the warmest southwest-facing slopes (McCune and Keon 2002). In conifer forests of Yosemite National Park, local topographic predictors, including solar radiation, explained relatively little of the variation in LiDAR-derived canopy cover and height when considered alone, despite the importance generally attributed to solar radiation and heat load (Kane et al. 2015). That study examined canopy cover and height, whereas the present study examines canopy-surface complexity. We therefore examine whether heat load is associated with canopy-surface rumple in high-elevation Cascade conifer forests. Including it alongside canopy height allows the elevation–rumple association to be assessed after accounting for both canopy stature and topographic exposure.\n",
    "\n",
    "These relationships must also be evaluated in the context of spatial dependence. Nearby forest cells may share ecological attributes not represented by the included canopy and topographic metrics, leaving spatial patterns in canopy complexity that measured predictors do not fully represent. Conditional autoregressive models represent such dependence through relationships among neighboring observations (Ver Hoef et al. 2018). Including a spatial component can change both the estimated associations with environmental predictors and their uncertainty, particularly when predictors themselves are spatially structured (Hodges and Reich 2010). Comparing spatial and nonspatial models therefore helps assess how the estimated elevation–complexity relationship changes when residual spatial dependence is modeled.\n",
    "\n",
    "This study examines how much of the negative relationship between elevation and canopy surface complexity in eastern Mount Rainier National Park is statistically accounted for by decreasing canopy height, topographic heat load, and spatial structure. Airborne LiDAR was used to quantify rumple, elevation, 95th-percentile canopy height, and topographic heat load. A series of Bayesian regression models were then fit to assess how the association between elevation and rumple changed when canopy height and heat load were included, followed by an intrinsic conditional autoregressive model representing residual dependence among neighboring cells.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Methods\n",
    "\n",
    "**Study area and LiDAR data**\n",
    "\n",
    "This study took place on a 7.4 × 16.7 km airborne LiDAR footprint on the eastern flank of Mount Rainier National Park, Washington, USA (@fig-studyarea). Forested cells in the analysis span 1,336–2,081 m elevation (mean 1,678 m) on steep terrain (mean slope 22.8°). Airborne LiDAR data were obtained via the Washington State Department of Natural Resources LiDAR Portal, which redistributes the USGS WA Eastern Cascades 2019 B19 acquisition. The dataset was collected between October 2019 and August 2020 under the USGS 3D Elevation Program LiDAR Base Specification 1.3 and was delivered as classified LAS 1.4 point clouds ([U.S. Geological Survey project report](https://prd-tnm.s3.amazonaws.com/StagedProducts/Elevation/metadata/WA_EasternCascades_2019_B19/USGS_WA_EasternCascades_2019_B19_Project_Report.pdf)). The acquisition was delivered at USGS Quality Level 1 (nominal pulse spacing ≤ 0.35 m). All point-cloud processing was conducted in NAD83(2011) / UTM Zone 10N. All raster products were anchored to a single survey-wide 30 m analysis lattice derived from the overall LiDAR bounds, with the 1 m canopy grid nested within it, so that terrain, canopy, and rumple layers share a common origin and no resampling was required at any stage. Processing and modeling code is organized as numbered scripts in the project repository. A statistical outlier filter using eight nearest neighbors and a 2.5-standard-deviation multiplier was applied to remove isolated returns. Returns assigned to low-noise class 7 or high-noise class 18 were also removed. Height above ground was computed from a Delaunay triangulation of ground returns and written into the point Z coordinate, yielding a normalized-height point cloud. These cleaning and normalization steps were applied to each LiDAR tile separately. Ground elevation was derived separately from class-2 returns as a 30-meter inverse-distance-weighted surface, generated per tile on the survey lattice and mosaicked. Slope and aspect were computed from the mosaicked terrain surface rather than within tiles, avoiding derivative discontinuities at tile seams. NoData gaps were filled by nearest neighbor before differentiation and masked back to the observed extent afterward, so gradients are defined everywhere without reporting values over unobserved terrain."
   ]
