    "**Canopy Cover**\n",
    "\n",
    "Canopy cover was computed from first returns of the cleaned, height-normalized point cloud in survey-aligned 30 m cells, processed in 1,200 m chunks that pooled all intersecting LAZ tiles, so that no step of the metric calculation followed tile boundaries. The proportion of first returns above 2 meters was then calculated for each 30 m cell. This first-return estimator of canopy cover follows [Korhonen et al. (2011)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rse.2010.12.011); the 2 m height threshold follows [Kane et al. (2015)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.foreco.2014.10.038).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Canopy Height (p95)**\n",
    "\n",
    "The 95th percentile of first-return height (p95) was used to represent canopy stature because upper-canopy percentiles provide a robust estimate of dominant canopy height while reducing sensitivity to anomalously high returns that can affect maximum-height metrics. For each 30 m cell, p95 was calculated as the 95th percentile of normalized first-return heights, including returns from the ground; in open cells p95 therefore also reflects canopy cover (Spearman $\\rho = 0.77$ between p95 and cover across the analysis cells). Moreover, p95 has repeatedly been used with rumple to characterize conifer stand structure ([Kane et al. 2010a](https://doi.org/10.1139/X10-024), [2010b](https://doi.org/10.1139/X10-064)).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Canopy Height Model**\n",
    "\n",
    "A raster of canopy height (CHM) was created by employing a layered-TIN approach inspired by [Khosravipour et al. (2014)](https://doi.org/10.14358/PERS.80.9.863). This was done in an effort to correct for a common issue with CHMs derived from airborne LiDAR, where artificial depressions, or “pits,” are created when laser pulses penetrate gaps in the upper canopy and return from lower branches, understory vegetation, or the ground. Before stratification, the highest first return within each survey-aligned 1-meter cell was retained. First returns were then stratified at height thresholds of 0, 2, 5, 10, 15, and 25 meters to construct successive upper-canopy surfaces. The 25-meter threshold was selected as the highest additional stratum that retained sufficient return density across the study area; substantially higher thresholds produced very sparse point subsets. Each stratum was triangulated. Triangles exceeding a maximum edge length of 5 meters for the base layer and 1.5 meters for elevated layers were discarded to prevent the TIN surfaces from bridging large unsupported gaps in the canopy. For each 1-meter cell, the maximum supported height estimated across all strata was retained, reducing artificial canopy depressions that can result from a single triangulated surface. To reduce computational intensity and minimize chunk-edge effects, CHMs were processed in 600-meter chunks with a 20-meter buffer on the survey lattice and then mosaicked together.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Rumple**\n",
    "\n",
    "Rumple was computed from the 1-meter CHM at 30 m resolution. Before surface-area calculation, the CHM was smoothed using a mask-aware 3 × 3 moving window inspired by the approach used in [Kane et al. (2010b)](https://doi.org/10.1139/X10-064). Only valid CHM cells contributed to the neighborhood mean, preventing cells without a valid canopy-height estimate from being treated as zero. A smoothed value could still be calculated temporarily for a missing cell from surrounding valid neighbors; these temporary values were used only to calculate local surface gradients and were excluded from the final rumple aggregation.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the smoothed canopy surface, the local surface-area multiplier for each supported 1 m cell was calculated as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A_f = \\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $z$ is canopy height. Rumple was then calculated for each 30-meter cell as the mean surface-area multiplier across all supported 1-meter cells within that cell,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "R = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n} A_{f,j},\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "providing a raster-based analog of the ratio of three-dimensional surface area to projected planar area ([Jenness 2004](https://doi.org/10.2193/0091-7648\\(2004\\)032%5B0829\\:CLSAFD%5D2.0.CO;2); [Parker et al. 2004](https://doi.org/10.1007/s10021-004-0136-5); [Kane et al. 2010b](https://doi.org/10.1139/X10-064)). A 30-meter cell was retained only when surface-area multipliers could be calculated for at least 90% of its constituent 1-meter cells.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Topographic Heat Load**\n",
    "\n",
    "Topographic heat load was used to represent variation in potential solar heating associated with terrain orientation and slope. Heat load was calculated at a latitude of 46.85° N using Equation 3 from [McCune and Keon (2002)](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1654-1103.2002.tb02087.x), which integrates slope, aspect, and latitude into a single index of potential annual heat loading. With slope $S$, latitude $L$, and aspect $\\theta$ in radians, aspect was folded about the northeast–southwest axis,\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A = \\left|\\pi - \\left|\\theta - \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\right|\\right|,\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "and heat load was calculated as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "H = 0.339 + 0.808\\cos(L)\\cos(S) - 0.196\\sin(L)\\sin(S) - 0.482\\cos(A)\\sin(S).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Slope and aspect were derived from the 30 m digital elevation model. Equation 3 is specified for latitudes of 30–60° N and slopes up to 60°; 30 of the 21,897 analysis cells (0.1%) had slopes above 60° (maximum 66°) and were retained. The resulting heat-load values represent potential topographic exposure rather than direct measurements of canopy temperature or moisture.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Non-spatial Bayesian Regressions**\n",
    "\n",
    "First, a structural forest mask was applied by removing cells with less than 10% canopy cover or p95 canopy height below 5 m, which retained 22,482 cells. The analysis was then restricted to the largest connected component of the 45 m neighborhood graph (21,897 cells; see Spatial Bayesian Model), so that the nonspatial and spatial models were fit to the same cells. Elevation, heat load, and p95 were standardized once, within these 21,897 cells, and the same standardized values were used in every model. Six Bayesian Gaussian regression models were then fit to evaluate how the relationships between rumple and each predictor changed when other covariates were included: rumple on heat load, elevation, and p95 individually; elevation + heat load; p95 + heat load; and p95 + elevation + heat load. Models were fit with Bambi ([Capretto et al. 2022](https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v103.i15)); no custom priors were specified, and Bambi's default priors were used. Each model used four NUTS chains with 1,000 tuning and 1,000 retained draws per chain. Residual spatial dependence of the full nonspatial model was assessed on posterior-mean residuals with Moran’s I ([Moran 1950](https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/37.1-2.17)), using a row-standardized binary 45 m distance band (rook and diagonal neighbors on the 30 m lattice) and 999 permutations, and with an empirical semivariogram (Matheron estimator, 20 lags to 1,500 m, spherical model with nugget, 3,000-point sample; [Matheron 1963](https://doi.org/10.2113/gsecongeo.58.8.1246)).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Spatial Bayesian Model**\n",
    "\n",
    "Because residuals from the full nonspatial model showed strong spatial autocorrelation, the final model included a spatial random effect. Given that the observations consisted of contiguous 30 m raster cells rather than irregularly distributed point samples, and that the inferential target was the regression coefficients rather than interpolation to unsampled locations, spatial dependence was represented directly through relationships among neighboring cells using an adjacency-structured Gaussian Markov random field (GMRF) ([Ver Hoef et al. 2018](https://doi.org/10.1002/ecm.1283)).\n",
    "\n",
    "The spatial prior was an intrinsic conditional autoregressive (ICAR) distribution, used as implemented in `pm.ICAR` in PyMC ([Abril-Pla et al. 2023](https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.1516); [Morris et al. 2019](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sste.2019.100301); Banerjee et al. 2015). Under an ICAR prior, each cell effect is, conditional on its neighbors, normally distributed about the mean of those neighbors with a variance inversely proportional to how many neighbors the cell has. Written jointly, this is equivalent to penalizing the squared difference between every pair of neighboring cells, so the prior favors fields that vary smoothly from cell to cell without specifying what causes the smoothness.\n",
    "\n",
    "An ICAR prior is improper along an additive constant within each connected component: adding the same value to every cell leaves all pairwise differences, and therefore the density, unchanged. A soft sum-to-zero constraint was therefore applied to identify the overall level, with standard deviation $0.001n$ following Morris et al. (2019). The field was drawn at unit scale and multiplied by a separately estimated standard deviation, the non-centered parameterization recommended in the PyMC documentation. Unlike the Besag–York–Mollié model, no separate unstructured cell-level random effect was included; with a Gaussian likelihood that role is played by the residual term $\\sigma$, and a separate effect would not be identifiable.\n",
    "\n",
    "The neighborhood graph was defined by queen contiguity (cell centers within 45 m). Because an ICAR prior requires one sum-to-zero constraint per connected component, the analysis was restricted to the largest connected component of that graph: 21,897 of the 22,482 forested cells (97.4%), with 74,989 edges and a mean degree of 6.85. The 585 excluded cells lay in 94 small components, 52 of which were single isolated cells. Excluding them was preferred to joining components by linking each stray to its nearest cell outside the component, which would assert adjacency between cells separated by up to several hundred meters and allow the prior to smooth across those fabricated edges. The nonspatial models used these same cells and the same standardization, so coefficients are expressed per standard deviation of the cells actually modeled and are directly comparable across the full model sequence.\n",
    "\n",
    "The final model was\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "y_i &\\sim \\operatorname{Normal}(\\mu_i, \\sigma), \\\\\n",
    "\\mu_i &= \\alpha + \\beta_p p_i + \\beta_e e_i + \\beta_h h_i + \\tau\\phi_i,\n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $p_i$, $e_i$, and $h_i$ are standardized p95, elevation, and heat load, respectively, $\\phi_i$ is the centered spatial field, and $\\tau$ is the spatial scale parameter. Priors were $\\alpha \\sim \\mathrm{Normal}(2,1)$, $\\beta_p,\\beta_e,\\beta_h \\sim \\mathrm{Normal}(0,1)$, $\\tau \\sim \\mathrm{HalfNormal}(1)$, and $\\sigma \\sim \\mathrm{HalfNormal}(0.5)$. The model was sampled with the nutpie NUTS implementation ([Seyboldt et al. 2026](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.18845)), using four chains, 2,000 tuning and 8,000 retained draws per chain, target acceptance 0.9, a fixed seed (1738), and nutpie's low-rank mass matrix adaptation (`adaptation=\"low_rank\"`). A diagonal mass matrix, the default, rescales each parameter independently and cannot represent correlation between parameters; because the elevation coefficient is strongly correlated with the spatial field in the posterior, its effective sample size under the default was an order of magnitude below that of every other parameter. Convergence was assessed by $\\hat{R}$ and effective sample size for all scalar parameters (intercept, coefficients, $\\tau$ and $\\sigma$), and residual Moran’s I and the semivariogram were recomputed from the posterior-mean fitted surface. The posterior-mean spatial field was formed by multiplying the scale and the field within each draw before averaging, rather than multiplying the two posterior means.\n",
    "\n",
    "Explained variance is reported as the residual standard deviation rather than as Bayesian $R^2$. Because the spatial model carries one latent effect per analysis cell, a conditional $R^2$ approaches one by construction and is not comparable with the nonspatial models; residual $\\sigma$ is on the scale of the response and is comparable across the whole model sequence.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Results\n",
    "\n",
    "**Landscape patterns.** The final analysis included 21,897 forested 30 m cells. Of the 22,482 cells that passed the forest mask, 585 (2.6%) were excluded because they formed small patches not connected to the main forested area, which the spatial model requires (see Methods). Elevation ranged from 1,336 to 2,081 m, with a mean of 1,678 m. Mean rumple was 2.24 (SD = 0.80; range = 1.03–7.07), while mean 95th-percentile canopy height (p95) was 18.0 m (SD = 8.3 m; maximum = 58.8 m). Rumple and p95 were strongly positively correlated (Pearson $r = 0.93$), and both decreased with elevation ($r = -0.55$ for rumple and $r = -0.58$ for p95). In comparison, the marginal relationship between heat load and rumple was weak ($r = 0.16$). These spatial patterns are visible across the LiDAR footprint in @fig-metric-maps, where lower-elevation portions of the study area generally contained taller canopies and higher rumple values."
