    "**Nonspatial Bayesian regressions.** All six nonspatial Bayesian models converged successfully. When modeled alone, elevation had a strong negative association with rumple ($\\beta = -0.443$, 94% HDI [−0.452, −0.435]), while heat load had a comparatively weak positive association ($\\beta = 0.130$, [0.119, 0.139]). Canopy height showed the strongest association with rumple: the p95-only model produced a coefficient of 0.742 [0.738, 0.745] and reduced residual $\\sigma$ to 0.305, compared with 0.668 for the elevation-only model and 0.791 for the heat-load-only model (@tbl-nonspatial).\n",
    "\n",
    "Adding heat load to the elevation model had little effect on the elevation coefficient, which changed from −0.443 to −0.436. In contrast, when p95 was included with elevation and heat load, the elevation coefficient shrank toward zero, to −0.022 [−0.027, −0.017]. The heat-load coefficient likewise decreased to 0.036 [0.032, 0.040] in the full model, while the p95 coefficient remained large at 0.724 [0.720, 0.729]. Residual $\\sigma$ showed the same pattern: it fell from 0.791 in the heat-load-only model to 0.303 once p95 was added, and adding elevation to that model reduced it only to 0.302.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Model | Canopy height, p95 | Elevation | Heat load | Residual $\\sigma$ |\n",
    "|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
    "| Rumple ~ heat load | — | — | 0.130 [0.119, 0.139] | 0.791 |\n",
    "| Rumple ~ elevation | — | −0.443 [−0.452, −0.435] | — | 0.668 |\n",
    "| Rumple ~ p95 | 0.742 [0.738, 0.745] | — | — | 0.305 |\n",
    "| Rumple ~ elevation + heat load | — | −0.436 [−0.445, −0.428] | 0.098 [0.089, 0.106] | 0.661 |\n",
    "| Rumple ~ p95 + heat load | 0.737 [0.734, 0.741] | — | 0.036 [0.032, 0.040] | 0.303 |\n",
    "| Rumple ~ p95 + elevation + heat load | 0.724 [0.720, 0.729] | −0.022 [−0.027, −0.017] | 0.036 [0.032, 0.040] | 0.302 |\n",
    "\n",
    ": Posterior means with 94% highest-density intervals for the nonspatial Bayesian model sequence, fit to the same 21,897 cells as the spatial model. Predictors were standardized within those cells; coefficients are in rumple units per SD of the predictor. {#tbl-nonspatial}\n",
    "\n",
    "**Residual spatial dependence.** Despite the strong relationship between canopy height and rumple, residuals from the full nonspatial model remained spatially structured. Moran's I was 0.380 using the 45 m neighborhood definition, with a permutation $p$-value of 0.001. The empirical semivariogram also showed increasing semivariance with distance, with the strongest increase occurring over the first several hundred meters and additional structure extending toward the kilometer scale (fitted nugget 0.069, structured sill 0.028). The fitted spherical range reached the 1,500 m maximum lag, so the range itself is not identified by these data and only the nugget and sill are reported. Thus, the measured covariates accounted for much of the variation associated with elevation but did not account for the spatial organization of the remaining residual variation.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Spatial ICAR model.** The spatial model converged with no divergent transitions and $\\hat{R} \\leq 1.01$ for all reported parameters. Canopy height remained the strongest measured correlate of rumple, with a posterior coefficient of 0.663 [0.657, 0.669] (@tbl-spatial), about eight percent below its value in the full nonspatial model. The conditional elevation coefficient, by contrast, was 0.017 [−0.004, 0.042]: small, positive rather than negative, and with a credible interval spanning zero. The heat-load coefficient remained small and positive at 0.014 [0.006, 0.022]. The estimated spatial scale parameter was 0.327 [0.314, 0.339]. Residual $\\sigma$ was 0.203, below the full nonspatial model but well above zero; because the ICAR prior penalizes differences between neighboring cells, the spatial field represents smooth spatial variation and absorbs only part of the cell-level variation, the rest of which is carried by $\\sigma$.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Parameter | Posterior mean | SD | 94% HDI | $\\hat{R}$ | ESS (bulk) |\n",
    "|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n",
    "| Intercept | 2.242 | 0.001 | [2.239, 2.244] | 1.00 | 69,296 |\n",
    "| Canopy height, p95 | 0.663 | 0.003 | [0.657, 0.669] | 1.00 | 9,240 |\n",
    "| Elevation | 0.017 | 0.012 | [−0.004, 0.042] | 1.01 | 704 |\n",
    "| Heat load | 0.014 | 0.004 | [0.006, 0.022] | 1.00 | 3,418 |\n",
    "| Spatial scale, $\\tau$ | 0.327 | 0.007 | [0.314, 0.339] | 1.00 | 1,835 |\n",
    "| Residual $\\sigma$ | 0.203 | 0.002 | [0.199, 0.207] | 1.00 | 2,429 |\n",
    "\n",
    ": Posterior summaries for the spatial ICAR model (four chains × 8,000 retained draws). {#tbl-spatial}\n",
    "\n",
    "The posterior mean of the spatial field (@fig-latent) showed coherent multi-hundred-meter patches of positive and negative spatial effects not explained by canopy height, elevation, or heat load."
