  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8030fc7c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "After inclusion of the spatial field, the positive residual autocorrelation observed in the nonspatial model was gone. Moran's I fell from 0.380 to −0.039. At $n = 21{,}897$ even a value this small is separable from the permutation null ($p = 0.001$), but it is an order of magnitude smaller in absolute size than the nonspatial residual and opposite in sign. Weak negative autocorrelation is what a per-cell random effect produces when it absorbs a little local noise along with the spatial signal, and it is not evidence of positive structure left unmodeled. The residual semivariogram was correspondingly flat: the fitted nugget fell from 0.069 to 0.028 and the structured sill from 0.028 to $1.6 \\times 10^{-7}$, which is effectively zero. With no structured component remaining, the spherical range is not identified and is not reported.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Sensitivity of the coefficients to the spatial specification.** The canopy-height and elevation coefficients behaved very differently across the model sequence (@tbl-sensitivity). Canopy height moved by about eight percent between the full nonspatial model and the spatial model, from 0.724 to 0.663, having moved only two percent across the nonspatial sequence before that. Elevation, by contrast, moved by nearly twice its own magnitude and changed sign, from a small negative value that excluded zero to a small positive value that includes it. Elevation is the smoothest of the three covariates, varying gradually across the footprint on the same scale as the spatial field, so the two compete to explain the same low-frequency variation; canopy height varies at much shorter range and is correspondingly less sensitive to how the spatial term is specified. This is the behavior described by Hodges and Reich ([2010](https://doi.org/10.1198/tast.2010.10052)), and in terms of spatial scale by Paciorek ([2010](https://doi.org/10.1214/10-STS326)).\n",
    "\n",
    "The fitted field is correlated with both covariates across the analysis cells (Spearman $\\rho = -0.35$ with elevation and $+0.32$ with p95), and the two coefficients shifted by similar absolute amounts (−0.061 for p95, +0.039 for elevation). Because elevation's conditional coefficient was already close to zero, a shift of that size reversed its sign. Its posterior standard deviation also quadrupled (0.003 to 0.012) while that of p95 did not change, the variance inflation Hodges and Reich ([2010](https://doi.org/10.1198/tast.2010.10052)) describe for covariates aligned with the spatial effect.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Coefficient | Nonspatial (full) | Spatial (ICAR) |\n",
    "|---|---:|---:|\n",
    "| Canopy height, p95 | 0.724 [0.720, 0.729] | 0.663 [0.657, 0.669] |\n",
    "| Elevation | −0.022 [−0.027, −0.017] | 0.017 [−0.004, 0.042] |\n",
    "| Heat load | 0.036 [0.032, 0.040] | 0.014 [0.006, 0.022] |\n",
    "\n",
    ": Conditional coefficients under the full nonspatial model and the spatial ICAR model. Predictors are standardized within the analysis cells, so values are in rumple units per standard deviation. {#tbl-sensitivity}\n",
    "\n",
    "**Is the spatial field an artifact?** Because the field carries one latent value per cell, it can absorb coherent patterns of any origin, including acquisition and processing geometry. Four checks were run against the fitted field, using the 1 km tiles in which the LiDAR data were delivered; each null distribution came from the same tile grid shifted by whole 30 m cells to 500 random positions. Tile identity explained no more of the field's variance than the shifted grids did ($\\eta^2 = 0.28$ against a null of $0.30 \\pm 0.02$, $p = 0.67$). Neighboring cells straddling a tile seam showed no step, for the field and for raw rumple, p95 and canopy cover alike: their differences were no larger than across the shifted grids' seams. Rumple and p95 were instead smoother across real seams than expected (across-to-within ratios of 0.94 and 0.91 against a null of about 1.02, $p = 0.002$ for both, which survives correction for the 17 artifact tests recorded); the field itself was unaffected ($p = 0.22$), but the seams are evidently not neutral (see Discussion). Boundaries of the 600 m chunks used to build the canopy height model showed nothing. The field did not track per-tile pulse density, measured as first returns per m² from the LAS headers (Spearman $\\rho = -0.28$, $p = 0.085$, $n = 38$ tiles). Delivered file size per km², a simple proxy for point density, did track the field ($\\rho = 0.41$, $p = 0.011$), but it also tracked canopy structure itself ($\\rho = 0.68$–$0.70$ with tile-mean p95, rumple and cover), because taller and denser canopies return more points per pulse. At the 1,200 m processing-chunk boundaries the field gave a ratio of 1.118 against a null of 1.022 ($p = 0.040$); this does not survive correction for the 17 tests, and it has no mechanism, since the products of that processing step are themselves flat at that scale.\n",
    "\n",
    "Part of the remaining spatial structure is measured canopy structure that was deliberately excluded from the model. The fitted field correlates $\\rho = 0.44$ with canopy cover and $\\rho = 0.44$ with canopy relief ratio, both computed in this study but omitted as covariates because they are strongly collinear with p95 and with each other.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "Canopy surface complexity declined substantially with elevation across the study area, but the model sequence showed that this relationship was closely associated with concurrent changes in canopy height. Elevation alone had a strong negative relationship with rumple, whereas the elevation coefficient became small once p95 was included and its credible interval spanned zero once spatial structure was modeled as well. Canopy height, in contrast, remained strongly associated with rumple across every specification. The results therefore do not suggest that elevation is unimportant to forest structure. Rather, they show that the observed elevational pattern in rumple occurs alongside an elevational decline in canopy height, and that once that decline and the residual spatial structure are both accounted for, no independent elevation effect remains identifiable in these data.\n",
    "\n",
    "The strong relationship between p95 and rumple is also consistent with previous work linking canopy height and structural complexity. Kane et al. ([2010b](https://doi.org/10.1139/X10-064)) noted that rumple tends to increase with tree height in conifer forests, while Gough et al. ([2020](https://doi.org/10.1111/geb.13180)) and Atkins et al. ([2022](https://doi.org/10.1111/1365-2435.13983)) similarly found strong relationships between canopy height and measures of canopy complexity, although their measures describe the arrangement of vegetation within the canopy rather than its outer surface. Some association is expected geometrically because taller forests provide greater vertical space over which crown heights and canopy gaps can vary; Kane et al. ([2010b](https://doi.org/10.1139/X10-064)) state that the surface area of conifer crowns typically increases with tree height, causing rumple to increase as well. However, p95 and rumple represent distinct properties of the canopy. Here, p95 was calculated directly from the distribution of normalized first-return heights, whereas rumple was calculated from spatial gradients of a pit-free canopy height model. Their strong relationship should therefore be interpreted as an association between canopy height and canopy-surface complexity rather than as evidence that the two metrics are interchangeable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Topographic heat load explained comparatively little variation in rumple. Although heat load showed a positive marginal association, its coefficient was small after canopy height and elevation were included and remained small in the spatial model. Heat load summarizes potential solar exposure associated with slope, aspect, and latitude ([McCune and Keon 2002](https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1654-1103.2002.tb02087.x)), but it does not directly measure temperature, soil moisture, snow persistence, or productivity. The weak conditional relationship observed here therefore provides little evidence that topographic exposure, as represented by this index alone, accounts for substantial variation in canopy surface complexity across the study area.\n",
    "\n",
    "Canopy height and topography did not account for all of the spatial pattern in rumple. Residuals from the full nonspatial model remained positively autocorrelated, indicating that neighboring cells tended to have similar deviations from the values predicted by the measured covariates. Adding the spatial ICAR effect removed that structure, and it did so while moving the canopy-height coefficient by only about eight percent — but it reversed the sign of the elevation coefficient, and that contrast is itself informative. Elevation varies smoothly across the footprint at the same scale as the spatial field, so the two compete for the same variation; canopy height varies at much shorter range and is comparatively insensitive. A coefficient on a smooth covariate is therefore only as stable as the decision about how to model residual spatial structure, while a coefficient on a rough covariate is not (Hodges and Reich [2010](https://doi.org/10.1198/tast.2010.10052)). Approaches that restrict the spatial effect to the orthogonal complement of the covariates have been proposed to address this (Hodges and Reich [2010](https://doi.org/10.1198/tast.2010.10052)), though their properties remain debated ([Hanks et al. 2015](https://doi.org/10.1002/env.2331); [Khan and Calder 2022](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.2020.1788949)); no such restriction was applied here, and the elevation coefficient is reported as specification-dependent rather than resolved.\n",
    "\n",
    "Part of the remaining structure is measured but unmodeled canopy structure rather than unknown ecology: the fitted field correlates $\\rho = 0.44$ with canopy cover and $\\rho = 0.44$ with canopy relief ratio, both computed here but excluded as covariates because of their collinearity with p95. Beyond that, spatially organized factors not represented in the models could include differences in disturbance history, species composition, stand development, soils, snow persistence, or moisture availability, although these mechanisms were not measured directly in this study.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several limitations constrain interpretation of these results. Rumple and p95 were smoother across LiDAR tile seams than across arbitrary lines, although neither showed a step there and the spatial field was unaffected. Outlier filtering and height normalization were run tile by tile without a buffer, and edge effects from those steps are one plausible cause; the field showed no association with pulse density, but an influence of tile-edge processing on the canopy metrics cannot be excluded. Flight-line effects within tiles were not tested, since swath boundaries do not align with tile boundaries. Rumple measures the geometry of the outer canopy surface and does not capture all dimensions of forest structural complexity, such as understory layering, foliage-height diversity, or deadwood. Because p95 was computed over all first returns, including ground returns, part of its association with rumple in open cells may reflect canopy cover rather than height alone. The analysis also relied on a single LiDAR acquisition and study area and lacked independent field measurements against which the derived structural metrics could be validated. In addition, rumple is scale dependent, so the values and relationships reported here are specific to the 1 m canopy surface and 30 m analysis grain used in this study. Future work could test whether the observed height–rumple relationship persists across additional forest types and elevational gradients and could incorporate field measurements, species composition, climate, snowpack, or disturbance history to evaluate the ecological processes underlying the spatial patterns observed here.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the analysis shows a strong decline in canopy surface complexity with elevation across the eastern Mount Rainier study area, but this marginal relationship is accounted for by a concurrent decline in canopy height. Canopy height was consistently the strongest measured correlate of rumple and the only coefficient whose sign and magnitude were stable across every specification; topographic heat load contributed relatively little; and substantial residual spatial structure remained after the measured covariates were considered. Explicitly accounting for that spatial dependence moved the canopy-height coefficient by only about eight percent, while the small remaining elevation coefficient changed sign and its interval came to include zero. That second change should not be read as an adjustment for unmeasured spatial factors (Hodges and Reich [2010](https://doi.org/10.1198/tast.2010.10052)): the evidence that canopy stature underlies the elevational pattern comes from the nonspatial sequence, and the spatial model shows how much a conclusion about a smooth topographic covariate can depend on the treatment of residual spatial structure.\n",
    "\n",
